## ABSTRACT

This dissertation develops a unified theoretical and architectural framework that proves two seemingly orthogonal problems—designing resilient extraterrestrial settlements and controlling self-organizing particle swarms—are isomorphic instances of the same mathematical structure: a distributed, modular system that learns in real time while maintaining provable stability guarantees.

**Three interconnected contributions:**

**(1) Modular Habitat Architecture.** A complete quantitative systems-engineering framework that transforms SpaceX Starship vehicles (9 m diameter, 1,000+ m³ pressurized volume, 150 t Mars payload capacity) into specialized, interconnected settlement nodes. The framework specifies: (a) a tripartite vertical zoning model (Lower/Infrastructure, Middle/Habitation, Upper/Command), (b) the Universal Docking Standard (UDS) with standardized mechanical (2.5 m diameter semi-kinematic latch, ±15 cm/±5° tolerance), fluid (5 types: air, water, thermal, utility gas, vacuum), power (480 VDC, 500 A), and data (12-fiber optic + CAN-bus) interfaces, (c) mass budgets for six module types (Habitation: 190 t; Agricultural: 140 t; Industrial: 160 t; Medical: 120 t; Energy: 150 t; Science: 130 t), (d) resilience metrics: Containment Latency T_c < 60 seconds, Self-Sufficiency Ratio SSR > 0.5 by Year 5, Maintenance-to-Living Ratio MLR < 0.4, and (e) psycho-spatial design principles grounded in environmental psychology and human factors literature.

**(2) Entropic Adaptive Learning.** A rigorous mathematical framework proving that real-time learning in physical systems is possible if and only if three conditions hold: (a) the perception module Φ is differentiable with bounded Lipschitz constant L_Φ (achieved via Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics), (b) the control rule f_θ is Lipschitz-constrained with L_f such that **L_Φ · L_f < 1** (ensuring contraction), and (c) the learning objective is the entropic risk measure J_β(θ) = (1/β) log E[exp(β R(τ))] (which focuses on discovering high-performing solutions). Under these conditions, Banach's fixed-point theorem guarantees convergence to a unique stable fixed point with exponential rate.

**(3) Unified Specification.** A technical architecture—the Chameleon Swarm algorithm—that applies particle-swarm learning principles to habitat resource optimization, enabling autonomous adaptation to resource constraints, failure modes, and human factors without central coordination.

**Novelty:** First proof that Lipschitz-constrained neural networks composed with differentiable SPH perception form contractions under an explicit, verifiable condition. First application of entropic risk-sensitive policy gradients to physical self-organizing systems. First quantitative trade study of specialized vs. monolithic Mars habitat architectures using current vehicle specifications.

**Keywords:** contraction theory, Lipschitz-constrained neural networks, entropic reinforcement learning, differentiable SPH, modular habitat design, Mars settlement, particle swarms, test-time training, self-organizing systems

