### 6.5 Resilience Metrics - Formal Definitions

**Definition 6.3 (Containment Latency, T_c).** Time from detection of a module failure to isolation of that module from the rest of the settlement.

**Specification:**
$$T_c := \min_{i} \{ t : \text{pressure door seals} \land \text{HVAC isolates module } i \}$$

**Target:** T_c < 60 seconds

**Mechanism:**
- Automated pressure sensors in each module (threshold: -2 kPa deviation from nominal)
- Redundant pressure door actuation: solenoid (primary) + mechanical spring-backup
- HVAC isolation: motorized ball valves (solenoid-actuated with manual override)
- Logic: all automated; 20 s response time; margin for manual confirmation

**Failure Scenario Test:**
- Micrometeorite punctures Agricultural module's outer hull (~1 cm hole)
- Pressure drops from 40 kPa to 38 kPa over 30 seconds
- Sensor detects at 38.5 kPa (t = 15 s)
- Logic activates sealing (t = 15-20 s)
- Door closed, HVAC valves shut (t = 20-35 s)
- Manual crew confirmation (t = 35-60 s)
- **Result: T_c = 35 s ✓**

**Definition 6.4 (Self-Sufficiency Ratio, SSR).** Fraction of mass consumed that is produced locally (not imported from Earth).

**Specification:**
$$\text{SSR}(t) := \frac{\text{Mass produced locally in synodic period } t}{\text{Total mass consumed in synodic period } t}$$

**Synodic Period:** 26 months (one Mars-Earth opposition cycle)

**Target Trajectory:**
- Year 1: SSR ≥ 0.10 (early ISRU propellant, first crops)
- Year 5: SSR ≥ 0.50 (mature agriculture, advanced ISRU)
- Year 15: SSR ≥ 0.90 (near-complete self-sufficiency)

**Components:**
- Propellant (CH₄, O₂): Sabatier reactor + electrolyzer; 100+ t/year by Year 5
- Food: Bioregenerative agriculture; 150+ t/year by Year 5 (providing 80% of calories)
- Water: 95%+ recycling + regolith ice extraction; net loss ~5% per year
- Manufacturing: Additive/subtractive manufacturing of spare parts; ~30-50 t/year by Year 5

**Definition 6.5 (Maintenance-to-Living Ratio, MLR).** Fraction of crew time spent on maintenance vs. productive activities.

**Specification:**
$$\text{MLR} := \frac{\text{Total crew-hours/week on maintenance, repair, troubleshooting}}{\text{Total available crew-hours/week}}$$

(Assume 100-person crew, ~168 hours/person/week available, accounting for sleep/R&R)

**Target:** MLR < 0.40

**Justification:** With MLR = 0.40, each crew member spends ~67.2 hours/week on maintenance, ~100.8 hours on productive/creative work. This balance supports both operational reliability and morale.

**Predictive Model:**
For a fleet of N modules with component reliability characterized by MTBF (mean time between failure):
$$\text{E}[\text{failures/week}] = \sum_{\text{components}} \frac{N_{\text{instances}}}{\text{MTBF}_{\text{component}}}$$

**Example Calculation (Year 1):**
- 10 modules × 100 critical components per module = 1,000 components
- Average MTBF = 500 hours (conservative for first year)
- Expected failures/week = 1,000 / (500 × 168 hours/week) ≈ 12 failures/week
- Average repair time = 4 hours
- Total maintenance hours/week = 12 × 4 = 48 hours
- Available crew-hours/week = 100 persons × 168 hours = 16,800 hours
- MLR = 48 / 16,800 = 0.0029 ≈ **0.3% (Year 1)**

By Year 5, as components age and MTBF decreases, MLR increases to ~15%, still well below target.

